## Warm-AOT summary

Ratios are **Fai ÷ peer; lower is faster**. Aggregates include only reviewed matched-kernel cases that clear the floor gate. They are point estimates for this workload set. Geometric means weight cases equally; p90 is nearest-rank across case ratios, not a latency percentile.

| Peer | Eligible / matched cases | Geometric mean | Arithmetic mean | P90 |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| rust | 44 / 44 | 0.500× | 0.500× | 0.500× |
| ocaml | 44 / 44 | 0.400× | 0.400× | 0.400× |

80 main workload/size cases are measured in total. Application cases retain absolute timings, with no compiler-kernel ratio. Warm measurements use 3 passes of 21 paired observations after calibration and warmup.

## Eligibility legend

`F/R` means Fai divided by Rust, and `F/O` means Fai divided by OCaml. Lower ratios mean faster Fai execution. The timing floor is measured using the same harness without the workload.

| Eligibility | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `matched kernel` | Peers share the reviewed algorithm and logical data structures, and all clear the timing-floor check. Their ratios qualify for matched-kernel comparisons. |
| `application` | Algorithms, containers, library policies or evaluation strategies differ. Absolute timings are shown; both compiler-kernel ratios are withheld. |
| `floor: <languages>` | Each named peer's measured harness floor reaches 10% of its elapsed time. Ratios involving those peers are withheld; another peer comparison may still qualify. |

For example, `floor: rust` suppresses F/R while F/O may remain; `floor: ocaml` suppresses F/O while F/R may remain; `floor: fai` suppresses both. `—` means an excluded ratio, rather than a missing measurement. Only eligible ratios from the main workloads enter the summary above; supplemental components are reported separately.
